# Oracle probe (L11.9, ADR 0186) — a container the program built rather than spelled out.
#
# The nesting itself is paid for: a list of lists is built, printed, compared, grown, and read back
# through a slot — `m[0][1]` and `d["a"][1]` print CPython's answer on both backends, because the
# literal the name was bound to tells the compiler what the tag beside that slot says (roadmap L11.1,
# ADR 0239 and ADR 0241). What still refuses is the read out of a container no literal ever described:
# `xs = []` then `xs.append([7, 8])` builds the inner object at run time, so the compile-time promise
# the read is granted by has run out, and the compiled leg declines rather than read a payload as a
# handle. The interpreter — boxed values, no static tag needed — answers every line, as CPython does.
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
print(m[0][1])
d = {"a": [1, 2]}
print(d["a"][1])
xs = []
xs.append([7, 8])
print(len(xs))
print(xs[0][0])
